Result: 'COMPILES OK' then 'ok 1 business-logic file(s) name none of the 36 banned constructs', EXIT=0. That file connects a TLS socket, binds a UDP socket, reads the wall clock twice, reads the monotonic clock and truncates a file.

WHY EACH ONE WALKS PAST, and why this is not merely 'a deny-list is never complete':

(a) SocketFactory. and ServerSocketFactory. were ADDED THIS ROUND specifically to close SocketFactory.getDefault().createSocket, and the State cell, scripts/check-io-seam.sh and AGENTS.md all say the family is closed. It is not: _shape() at line 66 puts \b in front of the owner, and in SSLSocketFactory the position before SocketFactory sits between two word characters, so no boundary matches. SSLSocketFactory.getDefault().createSocket(host, port) -- the standard way to open a TLS connection, and the way this project will open one -- is unmatched, as is SSLServerSocketFactory.getDefault().createServerSocket(). The entry closes the abstract base and misses every subclass, i.e. exactly the case the round claimed to fix. Verified directly against SHAPES: both strings scan clean.

(b) The X.now( clock family was never treated as a family at all. Instant/LocalDate/LocalDateTime/ZonedDateTime/OffsetDateTime are listed one per line (lines 76-80) while LocalTime.now(), Year.now(), YearMonth.now() and MonthDay.now() are absent -- all four verified as scanning clean. This is the same shape as round 2's blocking finding (listed family, unlisted sibling), and it is closable with a single shape the way Clock. was.

(c) new MulticastSocket( is a subclass of the listed new DatagramSocket( (line 88) and is unmatched; new PrintWriter(String), new PrintStream(String) and new Formatter(String) all CREATE AND TRUNCATE a file and none are listed beside the eight java.io constructors that are.

(d) A wildcard static import (import static java.lang.System.*;) defeats every owner-qualified entry, because the file then never writes the owner. Non-wildcard static import happens to be caught only by accident -- the import line itself spells System.currentTimeMillis -- which is worth knowing before relying on it.

The forward-looking case is (a): binstore-spi has no HTTP client today and no src/main file names WebClient/WebServer/HttpClient, so the module that eventually reaches a peer ingester over TLS will most naturally write SSLSocketFactory or a Helidon WebClient, and neither is seen. The disclaimer in the scanner header covers reflection, dependency APIs and the exempt module; it does not license the affirmative claim that the named families are closed.

