'ok 1 business-logic file(s) name none of the 36 banned constructs', EXIT=0. Both methods are refused by the PREVIOUSLY REVIEWED list and pass now. non-negotiable 2 forbids moving a threshold in the direction that weakens its gate; a deny-list is the threshold here, and two entries were removed.

The trade recorded in the backlog State cell does not hold up on either side:

- The claim '.toURL(, which is one of the two ways to obtain a URL' is FALSE. Class.getResource, ClassLoader.getResource and ClassLoader.getResources all return a java.net.URL while naming neither new URL( nor .toURL(, which is precisely what P2.fromDisk() exploits. A URL also arrives as a method parameter, as P2.fromTheNetwork shows.
- The false positive it was traded against is hypothetical: binstore-spi/src/main/java/binjava/binstore/BinStore.java declares get, getRange, stat, put, putIfAbsent, putIfMatch, multipart, list, delete, capabilities and presign -- no openStream -- and `git grep -E '\.(openStream|openConnection|toURL)\('` over all Java finds exactly ONE hit in the whole tree, the sample inside IoSeamGateTest itself.
- The replacement carries the IDENTICAL defect shape against a type that exists today. .toURL( is receiver-less in exactly the way .openStream( was, and binstore-spi/src/main/java/binjava/binstore/SignedUrl.java is `public record SignedUrl(String url, Instant expiresAt)`, where toURL() is the natural accessor to add. 'is not a seam method name' is a judgement about today's API, not an anchor, and it is the same judgement that was wrong about openStream.

Either restore the two entries with a receiver anchor (e.g. require a URL-typed or getResource-derived receiver), or keep the removal and say in the State cell what it costs -- but the current text records a strengthening where a measurable weakening happened.
